15
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:42.000
perception system? Well this is built to mimic human perception. So it is a component

16
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:49.599
that you can add to your actor which will go away and it will have a different setup

17
00:01:49.599 --> 00:01:56.640
for each different perception such as sight, hearing, touch and more. They all require

18
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.879
a little bit of a different tweaking but we're going to go through some of the most common

19
00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:07.060
ones and go through how to set them. Now this has two different classes that we're going

20
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:13.300
to use and need to know about. The first one is the AI perception component. Now this

21
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:22.099
is what you would attach to your player or your pawn or your character and this is going

22
00:02:22.099 --> 00:02:29.250
to be what does the work. It is a actor component. It needs to be attached to usually

23
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:37.360
a child of the pawn class. Then we have the AI perception stimulus source component.

24
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.960
Again another actor component but this doesn't do as much as the other one. It primarily

25
00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:50.639
allows you to register a actor as a stimulus source. We're going over that in detail.

26
00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:58.270
Now when we're working with AI perception most of the work is going to be done by overriding

27
00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:06.300
a number of events attached to the AI perception component. Now this has about four or five

28
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:13.259
events however a lot of them will fire at the same time because they will just provide

29
00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:19.180
a different subset of the data. So they will provide different amounts of data depending on

30
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:27.310
which one they are. For our needs we are going to be using but one of these and that is the

31
00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:35.939
on target perception updated. The second class is the AI perception stimuli source.

32
00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:43.169
The AI perception component registers things in slightly different ways but

33
00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:46.770
when we're talking about sight which is one of the most common ones

34
00:03:47.789 --> 00:03:54.909
it will automatically register anything that inherits from the pawn class.

35
00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.900
Now if you've got something that does not inherit from the pawn class yet you want it

36
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:07.259
to be seen and register with sight all you need to use is this component. We will be doing this

37
00:04:07.259 --> 00:04:14.860
too in an exercise where we will be setting up a pickup so that it can be seen and the AI can

38
00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:21.420
and interact with it. Now the other thing that's worth noting is some of the stimuli are not

39
00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:28.139
really registered in the same way by either being a pawn or having the source connected to it

40
00:04:28.779 --> 00:04:41.019
but by manually reporting an event. So in this case if you want to report a noise touch or

41
00:04:41.019 --> 00:04:48.139
damage event you're best off using one of these nodes. The perception event is talking about sight

42
00:04:48.139 --> 00:04:56.620
in this case which can sort of manually force the perception rather than adding a component.

